__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
# Parallel coverage fragments. pytest-cov under pytest-xdist writes one
# `.coverage.<host>.<pid>.<random>` per worker into the rootdir, and the exact
# name `.coverage` above does not match them. Untracked, they make
# `git status --porcelain` report a dirty tree whose path COUNT changes while
# the suite is still running -- which broke
# test_two_runs_differ_only_in_the_timestamp, since generate_evidence.py
# measures that count and two runs then legitimately disagreed. The generator
# was not non-deterministic; its input was moving underneath it.
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.mutmut-cache
.hypothesis/
# Bare, NOT `node_modules/` --- a trailing slash matches directories only, and
# the standard way to make one of this repo's ~40 worktrees usable is to symlink
# frontend/node_modules at the main checkout's 436MB copy. A symlink is not a
# directory, so the slashed form left it untracked-but-visible and one
# `git add -A` away from committing an absolute path into one machine's disk.
# Same for .venv, which was already being papered over by a bare entry in
# .git/info/exclude --- per-clone, never committed, so a fresh clone or a CI
# checkout gets the bug back. A fix that does not travel is memory, not
# mechanism. The bare form matches files, directories and symlinks at any
# depth, and a tracked file named node_modules is never correct either.
.venv
venv/
reports/
node_modules
playwright-report/
test-results/

# Scratch git worktrees created for isolated agent runs. They are full
# checkouts of this repository living inside it; committing one would nest the
# tree in itself and drag whatever an agent installed there into history.
.claude/

# Local sandbox evidence. Every `make sandbox-*` run writes argv, exit codes, durations
# and captured output here. It is generated, machine-local, and regenerated on demand;
# committing it would put a second copy of run results under review alongside the
# authoritative GitHub evidence.
.evidence/

# Vite build output for the learning-canvas frontend. Generated by `npm run build`
# in frontend/, reproduced from source on demand, and uploaded from CI as a run
# artifact rather than committed. node_modules/ above already covers the install.
frontend/dist/
# `tsc -b` incremental state. Regenerated by every typecheck and build; committing it
# means reviewing a machine's build cache.
frontend/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Mutation shard manifests. `npm run test:mutation` writes one per shard, and CI
# uploads them as artifacts so `frontend-verdict` can prove every stripe of the
# catalogue actually ran. They are per-run evidence, not source.
#
# Found the honest way: running the gate locally to verify a change left
# `frontend/mutation-shard-1.json` sitting untracked, and nothing in this file
# matched it. Its siblings were all already covered -- `frontend/dist/`,
# `frontend/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo`, `frontend/e2e/report/`, `frontend/ci-findings.json`
# -- so this was the one generated frontend artifact a `git add -A` could sweep
# into history. Ignored by glob rather than by the four current shard numbers,
# because the matrix width is a number in a workflow file and changing it must
# not silently reopen this.
frontend/mutation-shard-*.json

# Canvas browser-harness output. Every run writes measured metrics per project
# here; the numbers belong in the phase report, which quotes them, rather than
# in history, where a machine's timings would be reviewed as if they were code.
frontend/e2e/report/
.DS_Store
screenshots/

# Browser-automation scratch: timestamped console logs and accessibility-tree
# dumps written per run. Same reasoning as frontend/e2e/report/ above — the
# filenames carry a timestamp, so every session adds files that can never be
# updated, only accumulated, and none of it is reviewable as code.
.playwright-mcp/

# Next.js build output for practice-map/.
#
# `node_modules/` above is matched at any depth, so practice-map's install was
# already covered; `.next/` was NOT, and nothing else in this file matches it.
# It is 55 MB of generated output sitting untracked in a directory that is
# itself untracked, so `git add -A` — the obvious command for staging this
# work — would have swept roughly three hundred build artefacts into history in
# one commit. Ignored by pattern rather than by path, because the same trap
# waits in any other Next.js app added later.
.next/

# Official syllabus PDFs — large binaries, fetched by scripts/curriculum/fetch.mjs.
# The lock file (data/curriculum-sources.lock.json) IS committed: it is the
# provenance record of which document was reviewed.
data/source-pdfs/
data/almanac-ledger.json

# Deep-QA run output and Playwright artefacts. Regenerated on every run;
# committing them turns a test report into a diff nobody reads.
frontend/deep-qa-report/
frontend/test-results/

# Per-gate diagnostics. `scripts/run_gate.py` writes the complete captured
# stdout, stderr and a line-numbered combined log for every wrapped command,
# and CI uploads the tree as a run artifact so a truncated Actions log cannot
# destroy the evidence. Per-run output, never source. Same reasoning as
# reports/ above.
diagnostics/

# Property-test execution ledgers. Inputs to scripts/property_gate.py,
# deliberately outside reports/ so the gate finalizer does not read them as
# gate verdicts.
.property-ledger/

# Screenshot baselines are PLATFORM-SPECIFIC and are generated by CI.
# A macOS capture can never match a Linux runner, so committing one guarantees a
# permanently red gate. Ignored rather than trusted to discipline: see
# docs/visual-review.md.
frontend/e2e/*-snapshots/*-darwin.png
frontend/e2e/*-snapshots/*-win32.png
